    <!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
        <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="325HW7">
            <title>Math 325 Homework 7</title>

            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Due October 21, 2026 (submission on Canvas).
                </p>
<p>
In this assignment you will work more with Al-Haytham's identity to justify the results of Fermat/Roberval.
</p>
                <p>
                    <term>Generative AI is forbidden on this assignment. The goal is for you to understand the Method of Exhaustion prior to encountering it in ancient texts. Struggling with it with only your classmates will help your understanding.</term>
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <exercise>

                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            (6 points) Prove Al-Haytham's identity
                            <md>
                                (n+1)\sum_{i = 1}^{n} i^k = \sum_{i=1}^{n}i^{k+1} +\sum_{p=1}^{n}\sum_{i = 1}^{p} i^k
                            </md>
                            by induction. <em>Please do a careful proof by induction. Use a variable other than <m>n</m>, <m>k</m>, <m>i</m>, or <m>p</m> in your induction hypothesis and induction step.</em>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
            
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        (6 points) Finish the derivation from class using Al-Haytham's identity that
                        <md>
                            \sum_{i = 1}^{n} i^4 = \frac{n^5}{5} + \frac{n^4}{2} + \frac{n^3}{3} - \frac{n}{30}.
                        </md>
                        
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </exercise>
            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        (6 points) Do a proof using the Method of Exhaustion and the sum formula above that
                        <md>
                            \int_{0}^{2} x^4 dx = \frac{2^5}{5}.
                        </md>
                        Recall the steps:
                    </p>             
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Write down the left- and right-hand sums, show their difference is some multiple of <m>\frac{1}{n}</m>. Then argue this implies the difference between either sum and the integral is less than that.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Suppose the integral is less than the target value and let <m>\epsilon</m> be the difference. Argue that this means the right-hand sum gets larger than the target value then show that's a contradiction using the sum formula. Then do that again with greater and left. 
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                    <em> This should follow the proof we did for <m>x^2</m>, just be a little more annoying.</em>
                </statement>
            </exercise>

        </worksheet>
